{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"fc47d1e4-464e-4725-b795-8db513628c04","step":{"id":"s1","script":"A curve’s slope changes from point to point. We’ll find its slope at the marked point on this parabola.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Derivative = tangent slope"},{"op":"equation","id":"function","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"f(x)=x^2,\\qquad P=(1,1)"},{"op":"figure","id":"graph","region":"right","frame":{"x":[0,2.3],"y":[-0.5,5.2],"axes":"edge","x_label":"x","y_label":"y"},"marks":[{"kind":"fn","id":"curve","y":"x^2","domain":[0,2.2]},{"kind":"point","id":"p","at":{"on":"curve","x":1},"label":"P"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"fc47d1e4-464e-4725-b795-8db513628c04","step":{"id":"s2","script":"First use two points. The secant slope is the change in height divided by the change in horizontal position.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"secant","on":"graph","marks":[{"kind":"point","id":"q","at":{"on":"curve","x":2},"label":"Q"},{"kind":"segment","id":"chord","from":{"at":"p"},"to":{"at":"q"}},{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"p"},"to":[2,1],"stroke":"dashed","label":"h"},{"kind":"segment","from":[2,1],"to":{"at":"q"},"stroke":"dashed","label":"Δy"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"quotient","anchor":{"el":"function","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"m_{\\mathrm{sec}}=\\frac{f(1+h)-f(1)}{h}=2+h\\quad(h\\ne0)"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"fc47d1e4-464e-4725-b795-8db513628c04","step":{"id":"s3","script":"Move Q toward P. As the horizontal gap approaches zero, secant slopes approach one limiting value: the tangent slope.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"closer","on":"graph","marks":[{"kind":"point","id":"near","at":{"on":"curve","x":1.3}},{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"p"},"to":{"at":"near"},"stroke":"dashed"},{"kind":"tangent","id":"tan","to":"curve","x":1,"length":0.85,"label":"tangent"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"limit","anchor":{"el":"quotient","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"f'(1)=\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{f(1+h)-f(1)}{h}=\\lim_{h\\to0}(2+h)=2"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.warning","request_id":"fc47d1e4-464e-4725-b795-8db513628c04","code":"step_repaired","step_hint":"s4"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"fc47d1e4-464e-4725-b795-8db513628c04","step":{"id":"s4","script":"The derivative is two: the tangent rises two units for each unit across. Its equation uses that slope and the point P.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"rise","on":"graph","marks":[{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"p"},"to":[2,1],"stroke":"dashed"},{"kind":"segment","from":[2,1],"to":[2,3],"stroke":"dashed"},{"kind":"point","at":[2,3]},{"kind":"text","at":[1.5,0.7],"text":"run 1","size":"small"},{"kind":"text","at":[2.15,2],"text":"rise 2","size":"small"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"result","anchor":{"el":"limit","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"y-1=2(x-1)\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad y=2x-1"}],"checkpoint":null}}
